Now we substitute $\blueD{f(x)=2(\ln(x))^3}$ and $\goldD{f'(x)=\dfrac{6(\ln(x))^2}{x}}$ into the equation:

$\begin{align}
\goldD{f'(x)}&=\dfrac{3\blueD{f(x)}}{x\ln(x)}
\\\\
\goldD{\dfrac{6(\ln(x))^2}{x}}&\stackrel{?}{=}\dfrac{3\left(\blueD{2(\ln(x))^3}\right)}{x\ln(x)}
\\\\
\dfrac{6(\ln(x))^2}{x}&\stackrel{?}{=}\dfrac{6(\ln(x))^3}{x\ln(x)}
\\\\
\dfrac{6(\ln(x))^2}{x}&\stackrel{\checkmark}{=}\dfrac{6(\ln(x))^2}{x}
\end{align}$

We obtained the same expression on each side.
